.\" printf.1 -- maux's printf(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An
.\" original page (this project's own text, not copied from a
.\" historic printf(1)), so it carries no third-party notice --
.\" MIT like the code, same posture as xargs.1/cut.1. Rendered at
.\" build time to build/man/PRINTF.TXT and staged on the play
.\" image as /usr/man/PRINTF: `more /usr/man/PRINTF`.
.TH PRINTF 1 "September 14, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
printf \- write formatted output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B printf
.I format
[
.I argument
] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Printf
writes
.I format
to standard output, taking successive
.I argument
operands to satisfy the conversions
.I format
contains.
.I Format
is reused as often as necessary to satisfy every
.IR argument ,
but only while it contains at least one argument-consuming
conversion; a
.I format
with none is written exactly once regardless of how many
.I argument
words follow it.
An
.I argument
.I printf
runs out of before
.I format
does is treated as an empty string or the value 0, whichever
the conversion consuming it wants.
.PP
Within
.IR format ,
an unescaped
.B %
introduces a conversion and a backslash introduces one of the
escapes below; every other byte is copied through unchanged.
.SH ESCAPES
.TP
.B \e\e
a literal backslash.
.TP
.BR \ea " " \eb " " \ef " " \en " " \er " " \et " " \ev
alert, backspace, form feed, newline, carriage return, tab,
vertical tab. On this machine
.B \en
and
.B \er
are the SAME byte (CR, $8D) -- there is no separate linefeed to
preserve.
.TP
.I \ennn
a byte whose value is the 1-, 2- or 3-digit
.B octal
number
.IR nnn ,
taken directly after the backslash (no leading 0 required).
.PP
Every escaped byte, and every digit/sign/prefix a conversion
below manufactures itself, carries bit 7 set -- this machine's
own text convention -- so
.B \e101
prints as a plain
.B A
rather than an inverse-video byte.
.I Argument
text passed through by
.BR %s / %c / %b
is never touched this way: it already carries that convention
from the shell.
.SH CONVERSIONS
Each is
.BR % ,
zero or more flags
.RB ( \- " " + " (space) " # " " 0 ),
an optional decimal field width, an optional
.BI . precision
and one of the letters below.
.TP
.BR d " or " i
the argument, parsed as a (16-bit) signed decimal integer.
.TP
.B u
the argument, parsed and printed as unsigned decimal.
.TP
.B o
unsigned octal;
.B #
forces a leading 0 if the digits don't already have one.
.TP
.BR x " or " X
unsigned hex, lower or upper case;
.B #
prepends
.BR 0x / 0X ,
unless the value is 0.
.TP
.B c
the first character of the argument (nothing, for an empty or
missing argument).
.TP
.B s
the argument, truncated to
.I precision
characters if given.
.TP
.B %
a literal
.BR % ;
consumes no argument.
.TP
.B b
the argument, with ITS OWN backslash escapes interpreted (the
same set as above, plus
.I \e0nnn
for an octal byte, and
.BR \ec ,
which stops
.I all
further output -- this argument, any later
.IR argument s,
and any of
.I format
still to come).
.PP
.B \-
left-justifies within the field width (the default is right-
justified);
.B 0
zero-pads a right-justified numeric field, but is ignored once a
precision is given;
.B +
forces a leading sign on
.BR d / i ;
a leading space does the same but with a blank instead of
.BR + ,
and is itself ignored when
.B +
is also given. A precision of 0 on a value of 0 prints
.I no
digits at all for
.BR d/i/o/u/x/X .
.SH "16-BIT ARGUMENTS"
This machine's only integer size is 16 bits.
.BR d / i / o / u / x / X
each parse their argument into ONE 16-bit register --
.BR d / i
then display that register's bit pattern SIGNED,
.BR o / u / x / X
display the SAME bits UNSIGNED -- so
.B "printf %x 40000"
prints that value's hex bit pattern, and
.B "printf %d 40000"
prints the identical bits as a negative decimal number, the same
int16_t/uint16_t pun a real machine would make. A magnitude that
fits neither encoding at all (over 65535, or over 32768 behind a
leading
.BR \- ),
or an argument that is not a decimal integer, is diagnosed: a
message to standard error, the value 0 is used in its place, and
.I printf's
own exit status is 1 -- but conversion of the rest of
.I format
and the remaining
.I argument
operands continues regardless. A leading
.B \(aqc
or
.B \(dqc
argument is accepted (POSIX's own extension): it takes
.IR c 's
own value, low 7 bits, and is never a failure.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
0 if every conversion succeeded; 1 if
.I printf
was called with no
.I format
at all, or if any conversion failed as described above (a
diagnostic to standard error, the value 0 used, and the run
continues to completion regardless).
.SH FILES
/usr/man/PRINTF  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
echo(1), sh(1)
.SH BUGS
No floating-point conversions
.RB ( a / A / e / E / f / F / g / G )
-- this machine has no floating point. No
.BR %n$ -style
numbered arguments. A
.I format
with NO argument-consuming conversion, given MORE
.I argument
operands than it needs, is written exactly once (this build's
own reading of an unspecified corner -- some historic
implementations diagnose it instead).
